// ALG1 Unit 05 Lesson 02 Quiz
// Lesson: Factoring Quadratic Expressions
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.FND.8; MLA.A1.QE.3

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2+8x+15\).
{
=(x+3)(x+5)#Correct. 3 and 5 multiply to 15 and add to 8.
~(x+1)(x+15)#These factors multiply to 15 but add to 16.
~(x-3)(x-5)#These factors give a negative middle term.
~(x+8)(x+15)#These factors do not multiply to 15.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2-9x+20\).
{
~(x+4)(x+5)#These factors give a positive middle term.
~(x-2)(x-10)#These factors multiply to 20 but add to -12.
=(x-4)(x-5)#Correct. -4 and -5 multiply to 20 and add to -9.
~(x+2)(x+10)#These factors give a positive middle term.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2-4x-12\).
{
~(x-2)(x-6)#These factors multiply to 12, not -12.
=(x-6)(x+2)#Correct. -6 and 2 multiply to -12 and add to -4.
~(x+6)(x-2)#These factors multiply to -12 but add to 4.
~(x+3)(x-4)#These factors multiply to -12 but add to -1.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2-16\).
{
~(x-4)(x-4)#This gives \(x^2-8x+16\), not \(x^2-16\).
~(x+8)(x-2)#These factors do not multiply to \(-16\) with no middle term.
~(x+4)(x+4)#This gives \(x^2+8x+16\).
=(x-4)(x+4)#Correct. This is a difference of squares.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(5x^2+10x\).
{
~5(x^2+2x)#This removes 5 but misses the common factor x.
=5x(x+2)#Correct. The greatest common factor is \(5x\).
~x(5x+10)#This removes x but leaves a common factor of 5 inside.
~5x(x+10)#Dividing \(10x\) by \(5x\) gives 2, not 10.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q06::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q06] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.3]
If \(f(x)=(x+1)(x-7)\), what are the zeros?
{
=-1 and 7#Correct. Set each factor equal to 0.
~1 and -7#This reverses both signs.
~-1 and -7#The factor \(x-7\) gives zero 7.
~1 and 7#The factor \(x+1\) gives zero -1.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q07::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q07] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2+11x+30\).
{
~(x+1)(x+30)#These factors add to 31, not 11.
~(x+3)(x+10)#These factors add to 13, not 11.
=(x+5)(x+6)#Correct. 5 and 6 multiply to 30 and add to 11.
~(x-5)(x-6)#These factors give a negative middle term.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q08::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q08] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2-2x-35\).
{
~(x-5)(x-7)#These factors multiply to 35, not -35.
~(x+5)(x+7)#These factors give a positive middle term.
~(x-1)(x-35)#These factors add to -36, not -2.
=(x-7)(x+5)#Correct. -7 and 5 multiply to -35 and add to -2.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q09::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q09] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.3]
The factored form \((x-3)(x-8)\) has x-intercepts at:
{
=3 and 8#Correct. Each factor equals 0 at x = 3 and x = 8.
~-3 and -8#This reverses the signs.
~3 and -8#The factor \(x-8\) gives zero 8.
~-3 and 8#The factor \(x-3\) gives zero 3.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q10::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q10] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2+2x-24\).
{
~(x+4)(x-6)#These factors multiply to -24 but add to -2.
=(x+6)(x-4)#Correct. 6 and -4 multiply to -24 and add to 2.
~(x-6)(x-4)#These factors multiply to 24.
~(x+2)(x-12)#These factors multiply to -24 but add to -10.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q11::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q11] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(2x^2+8x\).
{
~2(x^2+4x)#This removes 2 but misses common factor x.
~x(2x+8)#This removes x but leaves a common factor of 2.
~2x(x+8)#Dividing \(8x\) by \(2x\) gives 4, not 8.
=2x(x+4)#Correct. The greatest common factor is \(2x\).
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q12::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q12] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2-25\).
{
~(x-5)^2#This expands to \(x^2-10x+25\).
~(x+5)^2#This expands to \(x^2+10x+25\).
=(x-5)(x+5)#Correct. This is a difference of squares.
~(x-25)(x+1)#These factors do not multiply to \(x^2-25\).
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q13::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q13] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2+4x-21\).
{
=(x+7)(x-3)#Correct. 7 and -3 multiply to -21 and add to 4.
~(x-7)(x+3)#These factors add to -4.
~(x+1)(x-21)#These factors add to -20.
~(x+4)(x-21)#These factors do not multiply to -21.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q14::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q14] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.3]
If the zeros are -2 and 5, which factored form matches?
{
~(x-2)(x+5)#This gives zeros 2 and -5.
~(x+2)(x+5)#This gives zeros -2 and -5.
~(x-2)(x-5)#This gives zeros 2 and 5.
=(x+2)(x-5)#Correct. \(x+2=0\) gives -2, and \(x-5=0\) gives 5.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q15::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q15] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2-10x+24\).
{
~(x+4)(x+6)#These factors give a positive middle term.
=(x-4)(x-6)#Correct. -4 and -6 multiply to 24 and add to -10.
~(x-3)(x-8)#These factors multiply to 24 but add to -11.
~(x+3)(x+8)#These factors give a positive middle term.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q16::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q16] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2+9x+18\).
{
~(x+1)(x+18)#These factors add to 19.
~(x+2)(x+9)#These factors add to 11.
=(x+3)(x+6)#Correct. 3 and 6 multiply to 18 and add to 9.
~(x-3)(x-6)#These factors give a negative middle term.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q17::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q17] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.3]
If \(f(x)=(x+4)(x+9)\), what are the zeros?
{
=-4 and -9#Correct. Set each factor equal to 0.
~4 and 9#This reverses both signs.
~-4 and 9#The factor \(x+9\) gives zero -9.
~4 and -9#The factor \(x+4\) gives zero -4.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q18::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q18] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2-49\).
{
~(x-7)^2#This expands to \(x^2-14x+49\).
=(x-7)(x+7)#Correct. This is a difference of squares.
~(x+7)^2#This expands to \(x^2+14x+49\).
~(x-49)(x+1)#These factors do not match the expression.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q19::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q19] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2-x-30\).
{
~(x-5)(x-6)#These factors multiply to 30.
~(x+5)(x+6)#These factors give a positive middle term.
=(x-6)(x+5)#Correct. -6 and 5 multiply to -30 and add to -1.
~(x+6)(x-5)#These factors add to 1.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q20::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q20] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(4x^2-12x\).
{
~4(x^2-3x)#This removes 4 but misses common factor x.
~x(4x-12)#This removes x but leaves a common factor of 4.
~4x(x-12)#Dividing \(-12x\) by \(4x\) gives -3, not -12.
=4x(x-3)#Correct. The greatest common factor is \(4x\).
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q21::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q21] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2+3x-28\).
{
=(x+7)(x-4)#Correct. 7 and -4 multiply to -28 and add to 3.
~(x-7)(x+4)#These factors add to -3.
~(x+1)(x-28)#These factors add to -27.
~(x+3)(x-28)#These factors do not multiply to -28.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q22::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q22] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.3]
The factored form \((x+6)(x-1)\) has zeros:
{
~-6 and -1#The factor \(x-1\) gives zero 1.
=-6 and 1#Correct. \(x+6=0\) gives -6, and \(x-1=0\) gives 1.
~6 and -1#This reverses both signs.
~6 and 1#The factor \(x+6\) gives zero -6.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q23::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q23] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2+12x+36\).
{
~(x+3)(x+12)#These factors multiply to 36 but add to 15.
~(x+4)(x+9)#These factors multiply to 36 but add to 13.
=(x+6)^2#Correct. \(x^2+12x+36=(x+6)(x+6)\).
~(x-6)^2#This gives \(x^2-12x+36\).
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q24::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q24] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8]
Factor \(x^2-6x+8\).
{
~(x+2)(x+4)#These factors give a positive middle term.
~(x-1)(x-8)#These factors multiply to 8 but add to -9.
~(x+1)(x+8)#These factors give a positive middle term.
=(x-2)(x-4)#Correct. -2 and -4 multiply to 8 and add to -6.
}

::ALG1_U05_L02_Q25::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L02_Q25] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.3]
If the x-intercepts are \(1\) and \(-3\), which factored form matches?
{
=(x-1)(x+3)#Correct. \(x-1=0\) gives 1, and \(x+3=0\) gives -3.
~(x+1)(x-3)#This gives x-intercepts -1 and 3.
~(x-1)(x-3)#This gives x-intercepts 1 and 3.
~(x+1)(x+3)#This gives x-intercepts -1 and -3.
}
